        "note": "Subjective\n\nNot provided\n\nObjective\n\nVital Signs: Temperature afebrile. Blood pressure 128/76 mmHg. Heart rate not documented.\n\nPhysical Examination: Faint crackles at the right base. Urinalysis negative, no nitrites.\n\nTesting Results\n\nUrinalysis: negative, no nitrites.\n\nAssessment\n\nPatient is a 71-year-old female with no past medical history documented in the available records who presented with community-acquired pneumonia. She was admitted and is now on day 3 of ceftriaxone therapy, with afebrile status overnight and improving cough. Physical examination reveals faint crackles at the right base, consistent with resolving pneumonia. Urinalysis is negative. Overall clinical trajectory supports continued inpatient management with current antibiotic therapy and close monitoring for further improvement.\n\nPlan\n\n1. Continue intravenous ceftriaxone for community-acquired pneumonia; repeat chest X-ray tomorrow morning to assess interval improvement.\n2. Restart metoprolol tartrate 25 mg PO BID; blood pressure well controlled at 128/76 mmHg on current dose.\n3. Assess for dysuria at the afternoon visit; urinalysis already negative for infection.\n4. Monitor clinical response to antibiotics and reassess disposition as clinical improvement continues.",
